WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.439 --> 00:00:24.130
In this extreme HDR selfie photo, both the
Pixel 3a and XS Max seem a little bit underexposed,

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:28.519
and the OnePlus 7 Pro is the only one that
gets the white balance spot on, but it doesn’t

00:00:28.519 --> 00:00:32.820
have as much detail as the S10+, so I’m
giving this one to the Samsung.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:37.550
In this one, the Pixel 3a has some pretty
harsh shadows, and the S10+ looks to be a

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.739
bit too cool on the white balance.

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:44.420
The XS Max looks really detailed in this photo,
but due to the clouds being so blown out,

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:46.280
this one’s going to the OnePlus 7 Pro.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:51.489
Moving to the rear side, both the 7 Pro and
XS Max look a bit dull and flat, and while

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:56.379
the S10+ looks very detailed, it’s looking
a bit dark and lacking highlights.

00:00:56.379 --> 00:00:58.890
The Pixel 3a looked the closest to real life.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:03.390
And if we crop in, we can easily see that
the S10+ has the most detail out of these

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:04.390
phones.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:08.480
In this portrait photo, we see that same detail
shining on the Samsung, with the Pixel 3a

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:09.750
right behind it.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.560
However, the Pixel’s white balance is way
too cool.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:17.400
You can see that the S10+ uses the regular
lens instead of the telephoto lens for portraits.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:22.610
The OnePlus looks a bit blurry or lacking
detail, and the XS Max gives us a nice balanced

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:23.610
photo.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:28.379
We see the same exact thing in this photo
so it may be due to noise reduction or a low

00:01:28.379 --> 00:01:29.379
shutter speed.

00:01:29.379 --> 00:01:34.690
The Pixel 3a is way too blue, once again,
and the S10+ is really smoothing out the detail

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:35.690
in the face.

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:37.780
We’ll go for the XS Max again for this one.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:42.050
We also noticed that you can’t adjust the
blur on the OnePlus 7 Pro, and the XS Max

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:45.739
blurs out the T-shirt, which looks a bit weird
but is actually realistic.

00:01:45.739 --> 00:01:48.479
No here’s a very tough HDR photo.

00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:53.119
The S10+ does the best at keeping the details
in the sky, and we actually notice a lot of

00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:57.550
detail in the hills and trees in the background,
and that’s because it has dual apertures,

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:03.110
so it’s shooting at F2.4 instead of around
F1.7 with the others, and the XS Max has some

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:05.250
very distracting lens flare going on.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:10.789
If we take a closer look, the Pixel 3a actually
does the best job at properly exposing and

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:11.789
keeping this young lad.

00:02:11.789 --> 00:02:16.200
In this photo, the S10+ overexposes by a good
amount, making the sweater look faded, just

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.629
like the OnePlus 7 Pro looks, without exposing.

00:02:19.629 --> 00:02:21.330
It just looks a bit dull.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:24.920
The Pixel 3a looks the best overall with great
detail and colors.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:30.110
In this portrait blur photo, the onePlus 7
Pro actually does the best at edge detection,

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:33.130
with the other phones struggling with a few
areas here and there.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:37.250
In this macro shot, the flowers were blowing
in the wind, so the phones had to freeze the

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:41.160
motion to get good detail, and the Pixel 3a
did this the best.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.280
Although the XS Max doesn’t look as detailed
because of the lack of contrast, I really

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.090
liked how it exposed the image.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:51.120
This photo was shot at maximum zoom, and we
can see that the Pixel 3a can’t zoom in

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.350
that far since it’s the only one that has
a single rear camera, and because of that,

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.190
you lose a lot of detail.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:01.740
The XS Max did pretty well but it’s lacking
contrast, and the S10+ is the clear winner

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.170
in terms of detail, although the colors are
oversaturated.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:09.760
In this macro shot, we tested minimal focus
distance and the S10+ was able to get the

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.280
closet, and it’s the most detailed as well,
with the Pixel 3a right behind it.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:17.900
Part of this tree was in direct sunlight,
so it was interesting to see how each phone

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:19.200
dealt with it.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.520
The XS Max was really flat, and the 7 Pro
and Pixel 3a crushed the detail in the shadows.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:27.459
The S10+ again has the most detailed camera.

00:03:27.459 --> 00:03:32.910
Two of these photos are shot using an ultrawide
lens, with the 7 Pro and the S10+, and it’s

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:37.380
interesting to see how saturated the s10+
makes this photo, but it still looks great

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:38.700
compared to the rest.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.530
One thing I also noticed was that the OnePlus
7 Pro’s ultrawide camera really lacks detail

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:44.560
compared to the S10+.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.940
It’s a night and day difference.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:51.480
You can see here that details are smeared
and the sky in completely blown out.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:55.840
The S10+ does an excellent job at this and
you can see a lot of blue in the sky.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.320
Switching to the regular lenses, the Pixel
3a actually looks the most detailed here,

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.040
followed by the XS Max.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.310
For some reason, the 7 pro and S10+ looked
a bit faded, with the least amount of detail

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:07.720
on the 7 pro.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:12.760
This is another tough HDR scene, and we can
see that the Pixel 3a blew out a lot of clouds

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:14.830
and underexposed the grass.

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:19.669
The XS Max chose to expose the scene very
well but blow out all of the cloud detail.

00:04:19.669 --> 00:04:24.449
The 7 Pro does a decent job but the sky looks
grey, almost like they brought the highlights

00:04:24.449 --> 00:04:26.190
down more than they needed to.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.190
The S10+ definitely wins here.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:31.340
And here’s the ultrawide versions of these
photos, showing that same difference in ultrawide

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:32.340
camera detail.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:36.860
In this one, it was up to each phone to freeze
the motion of the spinning the tire to capture

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:41.900
a detailed image, and the Pixel 3a again did
the best at it, shooting at a higher shutter

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.530
speed, making for a great looking image.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:49.389
And we tested the same thing here, with the
Pixel 3a giving a very detailed image, followed

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:55.250
by the XS Max, then the S10+, and the very
blurry looking OnePlus 7 Pro.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:05:00.160
This photo was shot testing shutter lag, hitting
the shutter button as soon as little Elon

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.650
got to the closest point to the camera.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:08.370
The XS Max features zero shutter lag, so it
was basically instant, followed by the S10+,

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:13.290
then the OnePlus 7 Pro, and the Pixel 3a,
which takes quite a while to snap a photo

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:14.990
after you hit the shutter button.

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:18.970
This one was shot in the shade with a bright
sky and lake in the background.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:23.870
The XS Max was really dark, and while the
S10+ looked really good, his face is really

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:26.180
orange here due to oversaturation.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:31.250
The OnePlus 7 Pro lacked quite a bit of detail,
and the Pixel 3a did the best overall.

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:36.320
In this photo, the S10+ looked way more detailed
compared to the rest, and the sand was actually

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.590
exposed very nicely at the same time, so I’m
giving it to the S10+.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:45.880
However, in this sunset cast shot, it’s
oversaturated and the water looks way too

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:46.880
green.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.740
The OnePlus lacked detail, and the XS Max
was a bit too cool, so this one goes to the

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:54.340
Pixel 3a for looking the most true to life.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:58.700
This scene was very tough since the sandy
beach is completely in the shade, with direct

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:00.940
sunlight hitting the buildings in the background.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:06.750
The S10+ oversaturates quite a bit, and the
building on the 7 Pro is a bit blown out.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:11.410
The XS Max looks great, but it’s lacking
some contrast, so this goes to the Pixel 3a.

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:17.340
Zooming in 3 times, we see the same results
here, except that the Pixel 3a loses detail

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:21.639
since it lacks a telephoto lens, giving the
win to the XS Max.

00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:27.960
Now at maximum zoom, the Pixel 3a looks very
pasty, and the S10+ is oversharpening a lot

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.180
of the details.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:33.750
The XS Max looks detailed, but there’s a
lot of noise, especially in the clouds, so

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:35.800
this one goes to the OnePlus 7 Pro.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:38.199
Here’s another one at maximum zoom.

00:06:38.199 --> 00:06:43.070
The XS Max again has a bunch of noise, and
the Pixel 3a just looks terrible.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:47.570
Although the S10+ looks to be more detailed,
if we crop in, we can see that it’s due

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:49.150
to oversharpening.

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:51.500
The OnePlus 7 Pro actually has more detail.

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:53.820
It’s just lacking some contrast.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:59.729
In this food photo, the S10+ looks really
good, but it just oversaturates the colors,

00:06:59.729 --> 00:07:03.380
leading me to choose the Pixel 3a despite
the cooler white balance.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:07.800
Here’s a dark indoor photo with some bright
details in the background.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:12.729
If you look at the texture on the couch, you’ll
see that the 7 Pro and S10+ are adding a ton

00:07:12.729 --> 00:07:15.740
of noise reduction, smoothing out the details.

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:20.680
The Pixel 3a looks great, but it’s way too
cool in terms of white balance, with the XS

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:23.630
Max being very accurate to what it actually
looked like.

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:28.060
Finally, we took a night time photo using
Night sight on every phone except for the

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:30.300
XS Max, which doesn’t have one.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:35.520
Although the Pixel 3a oversaturated the colors
a bit, it still looked the best overall, being

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:40.539
able to properly expose the shadows and highlights
without introducing much noise at all.

00:07:40.539 --> 00:08:17.740
The 7 Pro did a pretty good job as well, but

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:47.760
it was a bit too dark.

